WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.969
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Eddie Murphy.

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:06.172
So when did it occur to you,
"Hey, we have to go back.

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:08.274
We have to do a sequel
to this movie.

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:10.276
Enough time has passed"?

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:13.046
-Maybe about six years ago.

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:15.682
We had no idea we were going to
make this movie

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:17.350
when we finished
the original one.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.586
It was like, the story ended,

00:00:19.586 --> 00:00:21.454
and, like, they lived
happily ever after.

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:23.890
And then the movie,
over the years, became like --

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.826
it's the only one of my movies
that became like a cult movie,

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:28.995
where it has like an audience of
people that watch it

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:30.697
all the time and, you know,

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:33.499
some people get dressed up
on Halloween and [bleep].

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:37.136
There's a place that turns --
a restaurant that turns itself

00:00:37.136 --> 00:00:40.140
into a McDowell's restaurant
on Halloween

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.776
and they have, like, Sexual
Chocolate malts on their menu.

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:45.778
-[ Laughs ]
-So it's like, that's --

00:00:45.778 --> 00:00:48.681
I was like, "Hey, this movie is
a -- there's a cult following.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:51.584
You know, if we could figure out
a way to connect those dots,

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:54.086
we could -- there's an audience
for this picture."

00:00:54.086 --> 00:00:56.055
That was about six years ago.

00:00:56.055 --> 00:00:58.658
-Don't get me wrong.
You and Arsenio have aged great.

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:00.793
But it's really fun to see
the barbershop characters

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:02.695
'cause none of them
have aged a day.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.797
-No, they are older.
-You made them older.

00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:06.966
-If you watch --
Watch the original movie.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.300
You'll see they look much older.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:09.669
-[ Laughs ] That's good.

00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:10.870
-In the original movie,
they look like

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.506
they're in their,
like, early 60s,

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:16.543
and now they look like
they're in their early 90s.

00:01:16.543 --> 00:01:19.011
[ Laughter ]

00:01:19.011 --> 00:01:21.514
-One of the things that
must be so time-consuming is

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:23.716
sitting and getting into
the makeup like that.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.252
But the freedom of playing
those extra characters,

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:28.688
it must be worth the time
you sit in the makeup chair.

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:31.290
-It's the most rewarding thing

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:35.060
when you do those and it works
and it's funny.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.898
And the best thing, my favorite
thing is, lots of times,

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.200
people watch those movies
and they don't realize it's,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.135
you know,
us doing those characters

00:01:43.135 --> 00:01:45.671
'cause that's in the marketing
when the movie first comes out.

00:01:45.671 --> 00:01:47.140
But once it's around,
people don't know.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.508
They don't read the credits.

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:51.310
They just see it and, you know,
don't even realize it.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.913
That's my favorite, when they
don't know that it's me,

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:56.149
and, you know, I tell them,
"Oh, I'm that old Jewish guy."

00:01:56.149 --> 00:01:57.550
And they go,
"What are you talking about?"

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.718
"I'm the old Jewish guy,"
and they go "Ah!"

00:01:59.718 --> 00:02:01.621
That's my favorite thing.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.222
-Hey, I mean this sincerely.

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:04.623
This a free idea I want to --

00:02:04.623 --> 00:02:06.759
because as you talk about
playing multiple characters,

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.562
I think the Oscars should start
doing something

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:12.865
where they give out an award
every year for a movie

00:02:12.865 --> 00:02:14.300
that is 25 years old

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.503
that the Oscars admits
now deserved an Oscar.

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:19.538
And I feel like next year,

00:02:19.538 --> 00:02:22.474
"The Nutty Professor"
should get the first of these

00:02:22.474 --> 00:02:26.312
because I just feel like
it represents everything

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.481
that movies are supposed to be.

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:30.516
And when I think about
those dinner-table scenes,

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:32.585
and being lucky enough,
especially now,

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:34.287
when people aren't seeing
movies in theaters

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:36.622
with a bunch of people, like,
I remember being in a theater,

00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:39.092
like, collectively
laughing like that.

00:02:39.092 --> 00:02:41.594
And so that's my free pitch
to the Oscars,

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.663
is that you get the Oscar
you deserve for that movie.

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:45.632
-Aw, thank you so much, man.

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.567
That's one of my favorites, too.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:50.570
-The amazing thing is just
the rhythm and pacing of scenes

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:53.606
where you realize,
"Oh, it's just you

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:55.375
in five different chairs."

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:57.943
And the fact that you have to
figure that out.

00:02:57.943 --> 00:02:59.845
I mean, obviously a great deal
of it happens in editing.

00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:02.781
But when you actually see the
finished product, are you also,

00:03:02.781 --> 00:03:04.617
even though you were there
at different times,

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:06.252
are you blown away as well?

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:08.687
-Absolutely, yeah.

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:11.724
And like I said, it's rewarding,
but it's thankless

00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:16.729
because if it's done
really, really well,

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:19.064
you can't see all the work
that went into it.

00:03:19.064 --> 00:03:21.601
So, like, a scene around
the table that took, you know,

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:24.536
two weeks to shoot and that
whole sequence winds up being,

00:03:24.536 --> 00:03:27.005
you know, three,
four minutes long,

00:03:27.005 --> 00:03:29.676
and the audience isn't thinking
about any of that stuff.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:32.111
So it's rewarding but thankless.

00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:33.545
[ Laughter ]

00:03:33.545 --> 00:03:35.481
-That's -- I mean,
it was worth it.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:36.982
I want to tell you
it was worth it.

00:03:36.982 --> 00:03:40.319
I read that originally
Tracy Morgan was going to play

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:43.256
your son in this film
until you guys realized that,

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:44.590
I guess you only have about

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.926
a seven- or eight-year
age difference.

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:49.061
And it would maybe be
a hard sell.

00:03:49.061 --> 00:03:50.596
-The third draft of the script,

00:03:50.596 --> 00:03:53.265
we were still writing Tracy
as my son.

00:03:53.265 --> 00:03:56.135
Then we started going,
"How -- Does Tracy --

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:58.771
Would you believe Tracy
was me and Leslie's son?"

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.406
And it was like,
"No, I don't believe that."

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:01.874
[ Laughter ]

00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:05.377
We had to, you know,
turn him into the uncle.

00:04:05.377 --> 00:04:07.513
-I like that in a movie
where there is so much

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:10.182
that's unbelievable,
that's the one journey

00:04:10.182 --> 00:04:11.584
people aren't willing to make.

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:14.653
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.653 --> 00:04:17.123
They'll believe there's
a fictional African nation,

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:18.391
but they're like,
"I won't go for that."

00:04:18.391 --> 00:04:22.328
[ Laughter ]

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:25.765
Wesley Snipes is also
really funny in this movie.

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:27.499
-Wesley is amazing
in this movie.

00:04:27.499 --> 00:04:31.603
I did a movie last year with
him, "Dolemite Is My Name,"

00:04:31.603 --> 00:04:36.475
and he was a hysterical
and just wonderful actor.

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:38.911
We had really great
screen chemistry,

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:42.448
so I went right into this movie
with him, and so happy.

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:45.217
I originally was going to play
General Izzi,

00:04:45.217 --> 00:04:48.420
and I was like, "Wesley Snipes
would be so much better."

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:49.755
And he really, really --

00:04:49.755 --> 00:04:54.393
He is funny and menacing
and just a great actor.

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:56.929
-There are -- You know, I don't
want to give away too much.

00:04:56.929 --> 00:05:00.065
But I mean, it is really
fantastic the number of cameos,

00:05:00.065 --> 00:05:03.970
the number of iconic people
not just from the '90s

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.838
but from the last 50 years.

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:08.974
At this point, is it pretty easy
for you to pick up the phone

00:05:08.974 --> 00:05:10.843
and say to someone
like Morgan Freeman,

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:12.778
"I have a very good part for you
in this movie.

00:05:12.778 --> 00:05:14.847
Would you do me a solid?"

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:17.617
Does that happen pretty simply
or is it hard?

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:21.387
-We were like, "Do you think
Morgan would do it?"

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:25.758
And we went through the agents
and all that stuff.

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:28.260
And when he did it,
we jumped around and danced.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.563
It wasn't easy.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:30.563 --> 00:05:33.566
-I'm glad to know it's still --
there are still people out there

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:35.468
that are hard for Eddie Murphy
to get.

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:38.571
-Yeah, we didn't think of it
as being easy.

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.173
And we thought we were
the luckiest people ever

00:05:41.173 --> 00:05:42.941
when he said yes.

00:05:42.941 --> 00:05:45.511
-Well, I can only imagine
what it was like being on set,

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:47.680
considering how enjoyable
it was to watch it.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.114
I've got one thing --
I'll cut it out

00:05:49.114 --> 00:05:50.383
if you don't want to answer it.

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:51.517
You know my friend Neal Brennan?

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:53.019
He's friends with you.
-Yes.

00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:56.022
-He told me a story about how
Paul Mooney tried to beat you up

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:57.790
outside of The Comedy Store.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.959
-No, not try beat me up.

00:05:59.959 --> 00:06:01.127
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.127 --> 00:06:03.595
First of all, Paul Mooney
could never beat me,

00:06:03.595 --> 00:06:05.664
not even when he was young.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:10.303
In fact, most comics, as far as
comics go, I'm pretty scrappy.

00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:13.239
I don't know if I've met a comic
that, you know, could --

00:06:13.239 --> 00:06:16.041
at any rate, but no, there was
an argument because I went --

00:06:16.041 --> 00:06:18.911
they gave me the light the first
time I was at The Comedy Store.

00:06:18.911 --> 00:06:20.979
Paul Mooney and them,
they gave me the light

00:06:20.979 --> 00:06:22.949
because I was going on.
I just kept going and going.

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:25.217
I hadn't met Paul Mooney yet,
and they gave me the light.

00:06:25.217 --> 00:06:28.153
And that was, like, first year
"SNL," and I was killing.

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:29.488
And I was like,
"They givin' me the light?"

00:06:29.488 --> 00:06:30.823
And I was only there
for like 10 minutes,

00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:33.425
and I said, "Who's supposed to
be coming on?"

00:06:33.425 --> 00:06:35.327
They said, "Paul Mooney."
I said, "Who's Paul Mooney?"

00:06:35.327 --> 00:06:36.596
And they said, "Yeah."

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:38.297
I said, "Do you want to see
me or Paul Mooney?"

00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:39.799
And it was, "Aaaah!"

00:06:39.799 --> 00:06:41.734
So I did like another hour
or some [bleep].

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:44.636
So when I came off the stage,
they were, like, crazy.

00:06:44.636 --> 00:06:47.206
And Spoon, Witherspoon,
was in by the bathroom

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:48.908
at The Comedy Store,
and he said,

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:50.742
"Next time they tell you
to get off that stage,

00:06:50.742 --> 00:06:51.944
you better get your [bleep]
off that stage."

00:06:51.944 --> 00:06:53.312
I said, "Man, [bleep] you."

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:55.380
He said, "[Bleep] you
and [bleep] your mama, too!"

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.049
And we went outside
in the parking lot.

00:06:57.049 --> 00:06:59.318
Then Mooney came walking up,
and he's like,

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:00.686
"Excuse me, homey.

00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:03.055
Yes, you have a routine about
'The Amityville Horror'

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:04.690
about a ghost in the house?"
and I was like, "Yes."

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.325
He said, "Well, I have a routine
that's very similar.

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:07.760
Let me hear your routine,
homey."

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.728
And I was like, "I'm not doing
my [bleep] routine

00:07:09.728 --> 00:07:10.796
in the parking lot."

00:07:10.796 --> 00:07:12.098
And he said, "[Bleep] you."

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:13.365
And I was with John Landis.

00:07:13.365 --> 00:07:14.733
He said, "[Bleep] you."

00:07:14.733 --> 00:07:16.302
And he said, "And [bleep] you
too, white man.

00:07:16.302 --> 00:07:18.771
If I had a knife -- if I had a
gun, I'd shoot the both of you."

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:21.140
[ Laughter ]

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:23.209
That was the first night
I met Paul Mooney.

00:07:23.209 --> 00:07:26.446
And then Richard -- then when
Richard heard about it,

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:31.183
he took Mooney and Spoon,
and we took --

00:07:31.183 --> 00:07:33.953
he made them come to the
main room at The Comedy Store

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:38.224
the next night, and -- after
hours -- and apologize to me.

00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:39.458
-Wow.

00:07:39.458 --> 00:07:41.260
-Richard made them apologize.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.863
He was like, "You guys are
just being mean to that kid

00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:46.498
because he's a new, hot [bleep]
and y'all was jealous.

00:07:46.498 --> 00:07:48.201
And [bleep] that.
Y'all apologize."

00:07:48.201 --> 00:07:49.735
Richard made them apologize.

00:07:49.735 --> 00:07:51.570
Richard was always so nice.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:54.373
And then Mooney became a great
friend, and Spoon as well.

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:56.976
But the very first night
I met them, Spoon was like,

00:07:56.976 --> 00:07:58.377
"[Bleep] you and your mama!"

00:07:58.377 --> 00:08:02.081
[ Laughter ]

00:08:02.081 --> 00:08:05.651
-Well, needless to say,
I am glad I asked.

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:07.920
[ Laughter ]

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.621
Hey, thanks so much, Eddie.

00:08:09.621 --> 00:08:10.790
Please come back soon.

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:12.325
-Okay, Seth.

00:08:12.325 --> 00:08:15.594
When it's all safe to come back,
I'll come check you out, bro.

00:08:15.594 --> 00:08:17.563
-We'd really like that.

00:08:17.563 --> 00:08:19.031
Thank you so much.

00:08:19.031 --> 00:08:21.367
"Coming 2 America" is currently
streaming on Amazon Prime Video.

